 that swimming being ","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"soGH1GJiMY"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"oriented","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"IqmKL06mGI"}],"key":"BcRnrFpLPg"},{"type":"text","value":".\nThat is, swimming is primarily helpful if it results in sustained movement in a particular direction, especially upwards.\nEarly-stage larvae typically do not have sophisticated active responses to remain oriented in turbulence and shear.","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"r4rpcjkDVw"},{"type":"footnoteReference","identifier":"adv","label":"adv","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"enumerator":"1","key":"On735lLl05"},{"type":"text","value":"\nInstead, they usually rely on passive stability – that is, on the tendency to reorient so that their ","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"b1GqfALlRj"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"C_{buoy}","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{buoy}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9694em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">b</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">u</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">oy</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"xi6yAppSq4"},{"type":"text","value":" is exactly above the ","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"qNvBKjLjrd"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"C_{mass}","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{mass}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">ma</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">ss</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"RxmvGGL8jM"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"YwyLklJfEH"}],"key":"DoPTqSmlmT"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This suggests the hypothesis that, analogously to a “standard” for swimming performance in term of speed, marine invertebrate larvae may be subject to an additional swimming performance standard in terms of passive stability.\nBecause passive stability is a direct consequence of the distribution of excess density within an embryo, its size and other morphological traits, this hypothesis suggests an additional set of morphological constraints on larval life histories:","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"sUmUBYUDz8"}],"key":"vZhe26knn6"},{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Larval morphologies must provide passive stability in excess of the torques imposed on them by turbulence and shear.","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"CmcmkIHDys"}],"key":"ZmBDOofpjZ"}],"key":"EMhzcn3Qpz"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"If this hypothesis has validity, a futher hypothesis follows that these morphological constraints vary across habitats with different levels of turbulence and shear.\nFor example, the requirements for stability in energetic surface waters, where turbulence and shear are often relatively intense, may be much more stringent than the corresponding requirements in the relatively quiescent deep sea.\nThe different requirements might be reflected in different larval morphologies for taxa living in specific habitats.","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"DhHplUyEL6"}],"key":"fWRINiTcac"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It’s important to recognize that hypothetical swimming performance standards for speed and for stability may impose very different or even contradictory constraints.\nIt’s to be expected that torques imposed by shear and turbulence will be larger on embryos with larger diameters and surface areas.\nIf so, it may turn out that the decreases in average excess densities that enhance swimming speed by adding ciliated surface area have the side effect of decreasing effective stability.\nThat is, there may be combinations of environmental turbulence and shear with larval size in which morphologies are highly constrained, or even in which ","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"T4IUCMjnN5"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"no morphological variant results in a viably swimming larva","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"tW2uuwAd0B"}],"key":"OcNvRqr8iL"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"fEd3riENiZ"}],"key":"PxmzMxa0TA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The activities in this Unit use a simplified model of early larval swimming to quantitatively assess the consequences of hypothetical “standards” for speed and stability, focusing on possible morphological constraints and their possible implications for marine invertebrate life histories.","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"ObFpz177G5"}],"key":"OOBS8SMUEw"},{"type":"footnoteDefinition","identifier":"adv","label":"adv","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is not true of many more developed larval forms, which can have sensory and behavioral responses leading to active orientation. However, some larval forms continue to rely mostly or entirely on passive stability throughout their development.","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"key":"Ut0DttYKPM"}],"key":"aXDR7zJhQY"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"m4yc5uRV2p"}],"key":"MT77qtd6wX"}],"key":"wgDWnZRvM0"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"📖️️  Size-dependent changes in excess density","url":"/excessmass","group":"Biomechanics"},"next":{"title":"🛼 Morphologies that meet the challenges of early larval swimming","url":"/chimeraactivity","group":"Biomechanics"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3002"}